My #EY Interview Experience – DevOps Engineer
Recently, I went through the interview process for a DevOps Engineer role. I wanted to share my experience, as it might help others preparing for similar opportunities.
The entire process was smooth, well-structured, and professional. The HR team was supportive and transparent throughout.
Here’s a round-wise breakdown:
 
🟢 Round 1: Screening Round (15 Minutes)
This was a technical screening round primarily focused on Kubernetes fundamentals and CI/CD basics.
🔹 Kubernetes
Taints and Tolerations
Node Affinity
Kubernetes Architecture Components
Control Plane vs Worker Nodes
🔹 CI/CD
Stages in my pipeline
Continuous Feedback
Continuous Delivery concept
🔹 Terraform
Securing Terraform state files
Remote backend configuration
What is null_resource?
🔹 Tools Discussion
Tools I’ve worked with in real-time projects
The discussion was interactive, and I was moved to the L1 round.
 
🟢 Round 2: L1 Technical Round (30 Minutes – Internal Panel)
Focus Areas: Kubernetes, Docker, Terraform, Ansible, Jenkins
🔹 Kubernetes
Deployment strategies (Rolling, Blue-Green, Canary)
What is a DaemonSet?
Taints & Tolerations (deeper discussion)
Kubernetes Architecture
🔹 Docker
Docker networking concepts
Multi-stage Dockerfile – why and how
Writing optimized Dockerfiles
Docker image security best practices
🔹 CI/CD
Tools used
Pipeline stages
Continuous Delivery implementation
Rollback strategies in case of deployment failure
🔹 AWS (Scenario-Based)
VPC-related scenario
S3 architecture discussion
I also asked about project type, team structure, and tooling ecosystem. After this round, my profile was forwarded for the client discussion.
 
🟢 Round 3: Client Round (30 Minutes – Scheduled at Night 10:30 PM)
This round was more scenario-based and focused heavily on real-time project experience.
🔹 Infrastructure & Architecture
Complete infra architecture explanation
Microservices vs Monolithic architecture
Role of DevOps in the project lifecycle
End-to-end deployment flow
🔹 Security & Traffic Handling
Handling high or malicious traffic from specific regions
 (WAF, Security Groups, rate limiting, etc.)
🔹 DNS & Networking
Route 53 working
End-to-end DNS resolution flow
User request lifecycle
🔹 Kubernetes (Practical)
SSL certificate management
Certificate expiration handling
Common production issues and resolutions
🔹 CI/CD Troubleshooting
Handling failed pipeline stages
Debugging approach and team collaboration
This round focused more on practical exposure and problem-solving ability rather than just definitions.

🎯 Key Takeaways
✅ Strong Kubernetes fundamentals are essential
✅ Be clear about real-time project architecture
✅ Understand security and traffic-handling concepts
✅ Practice explaining end-to-end flow confidently
✅ Scenario-based preparation is extremely important
